      "writtenOn": "2023-02-28T20:57:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s a bit odd.  Why would a mutex that someone is waiting on be dropped?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 30,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "812da6a5a1dc0fcdd61dc743003f4bd073308de1",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1f05704c_aa4ca12a",
        "filename": "system/rust/src/core/shared_mutex.rs",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1958203
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-02-28T22:51:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If one task wants to send an indication, it calls `mutex.lock()`. But the mutex is owned by the `AttServerBearer`, and that can be dropped if the peer disconnects. So then the first task will unblock with `None`, since the IndicationHandler is gone.\n\nIf the task acquires the `IndicationHandler`, then blocks for some other reason (e.g. waiting for the confirmation), and _then_ the peer disconnects, we can\u0027t kill the IndicationHandler since it is currently owned by the task. Hence we get this lifetime extension behavior.",
      "parentUuid": "8a411276_f8008142",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 30,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "812da6a5a1dc0fcdd61dc743003f4bd073308de1",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
